    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/University-Dining-Supervisor_R-02408": {
      "title": "University Dining Supervisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: University Dining Supervisor Compensation: $43,888.00 Department: North Dining About this opportunity: Ball State University has a professional leadership opportunity available in Ball State University Dining that offers an excellent benefits package, paid time off and the opportunity for work/life balance. The University Dining Supervisor position is responsible for directing, overseeing, and leading all food and beverage operations within Ball State University Dining. This position is an evening/closing supervisor. The successful candidate will lead and direct subordinates resulting in good organization and minimal stress, promote teamwork, and execute excellent customer service that exceeds customer expectations. To learn more about Ball State University Dining, please visit our website at: http://www.bsu.edu/campuslife/dining . This position supervises full-time and part-time food service workers, cashiers, custodians, group leaders and student employees. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function: Under the direction of the General Manager, oversee and supervise employees in achieving excellence in customer satisfaction, food quality, service of food, employee efficiency and productivity, financial goals, and compliance with safety and sanitation standards, HACCP standards, Indiana Food Service Code regulations, and rules, policies and procedures of Ball State University: additional functions include but are not limited to food service forecasting and record keeping, physical inventory, subordinate training, food and supply purchasing, communication, executing promotions and advertisements, complying with branded concept license and franchise agreements and standards of operations, executing detailed plans and arrangements, contributing to menu, recipe, and concept development, and participating on a limited basis in preparation and serving of food or cashiering. Duties and Responsibilities: 1. Lead and supervise employees in executing correct, timely procedures in meeting license/franchise and department standards in relation to the preparation and service of all menu items, including but not limited to recipe preparation, food presentation, quality control, customer service, and sanitation and safety as defined by BSU manuals, handbooks, HACCP standards, Indiana Food Code, NRA ServSafe, and other industry standards; employ excellence in management techniques to exhibit evidence of teamwork and execution of excellent customer service that exceeds customer expectations in such areas, including but not limited to courtesy, speed of service, sense of urgency, product consistency, demonstrating a passion for excellence, utilizing fun and humor when appropriate, innovation and creativity, and other criteria. 2. Orient, coach, and train employees resulting in well-trained employees who consistently exhibit excellence in job knowledge and performance; ensure employees are well trained before working in any job assignment; provide accurate, balanced (positive and negative) feedback to employees regarding job performance and follow up on corrective measures; complete and maintain file documentation for all orientation, coaching, and training of each employee according to departmental procedures. 3. Count safe and audit safe record; audit cashier reports and submit thorough, accurate, cashier check-out reports to bookkeeper; properly report and account for shortages in safe or deposits of sales; complete documentation, report all unaccounted monies (over $20.00) immediately to the General Manager and/or University Police; check and approve all cashier refunds to customers and audit the no sale transactions of each cashier to prevent cashier theft; maintain accurate and up-to-date meal card system, including but not limited to reporting lost cards, verify account status, and overseeing the recording of off line transactions; administer university and departmental policies as they relate to money handling and reporting; ensure cashiers properly ring sales to record product movement and product value related to each brand upon which royalty payments to brand licensors or franchisors are based. 4. Utilize the Cbord Menu Management computer system to prepare menu item forecasts to (a) determine production of foods for service, and (b) correctly submit service statistics for historical data via computer record; under firm deadlines, scrutinize purchase requisitions and approve amounts of foods and supplies to be ordered based upon forecasted projections of sales and menu items, inventory levels, and other indicators; manage purchasing assignment with the utmost in business ethics and following policies and procedures of Ball State University; maintain appropriate inventory levels of foods and supplies as assigned, with neither being over or under-stocked; complete weekly inventory of foods and/or supplies with a high degree of accuracy. 5. Oversee and maintain adequate staffing following University procedures to schedule replacement employees; complete documentation related to scheduling regular and overtime staff; maintain correct telephone and address lists of all employees to facilitate this task; demonstrate a commitment to affirmative action and equal employment opportunity; communicate with peers and subordinates in a thorough, detailed, timely even-handed manner resulting in effective personal contact and/or the completion of management log entries that communicate necessary information to peers; attend shift meetings, unit and departmental management staff meetings, and relate telephone messages and/or other information accurately and in a timely manner; maintain confidentiality and ethical practices as relates to communications; in stressful situations, remain, calm, in control, and have a positive impact on others; manage disagreements effectively and create an environment where peers, employees, and customers are treated with dignity and respect and where rules, policies, and procedures are consistently administered. 6. Evaluate, investigate, and secure approval of General Manager and the Assistant Director of Personnel, or the Director, then issue warranted counseling or discipline to employees in a timely manner according to university policies and procedures; submit written documentation of all investigations, interviews, meetings, or conversations related to the situation in addition to copies of the properly issued disciplinary forms; accurately administer the union agreement and addenda thereto (including absence and tardiness policies and required record-keeping). 7. Unlock facility for the day’s operations and/or secure the facility for the periods of closure; observe safety and security precautions to prevent personal or employee injury, and theft of university or employee property; maintain personal and unit keys in a safe manner to prevent unauthorized use. 8. Establish, evaluate, and execute personal and unit goals, and contribute to departmental goal setting and achievement; serve in a contributory and effective manner on teams, committees, and task forces; demonstrate a professional appearance; wear clean, well-pressed clothing daily; follow prescribed dress code guidelines as outlined in departmental policies, rules, and regulations. 9. Evaluate employee performance and complete timely formal appraisal forms impartially and in an unbiased manner; submit draft appraisals for the approval of the General Manager; properly conduct the performance review meetings with employees; route completed forms in a timely manner. 10. Contribute to optimizing profitability through effective monitoring and supervision of food waste, portion control, inventory management, key control, theft and security controls, proper charging of sales to customers and employees by cashiers, and employees as they use and care for the facility, equipment, and furnishings; assist in evaluating food, labor, and other controllable costs; implement corrective measures as needed. 11. Collaborate in executing an effective marketing and promotion program for the unit in conjunction with the General Manager, approval from the Assistant Director of Personnel, and Assistant Director of Operations. 12. Provide planning of standard and custom menus, submit recipes for new menu items, and assist with development of sales mix for the unit; assist in planning and executing special events, catered events, and other non-routine food services. 13. Coordinate an effective laundry procedure to ensure proper supply of clean linen; maintain neat, accurate, require records; report maintenance and repair needs of building and equipment in a timely manner. 14. Perform food preparation, service and cashiering duties in emergency situations, and/or for short work periods to meet customer demand. 15. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: -Associates degree from an accredited institution or equivalent combination of education, credentials, certification, and experience. -At least one year of direct or related work experience (exclusive of teaching) in commercial or institutional food service, with demonstrated leadership and dynamic team building skills. -Skills in oral and written communication, strong analytical and problem-solving skills. Preferred Qualifications: -Bachelor's degree from an accredited institution. -Five years of direct or related work experience (exclusive of teaching) in commercial or institutional food service, with demonstrated leadership and dynamic team building skills supervisory experience. How to apply: For inquiries, please contact: University Dining at 765-285-1967. Supporting Documents Upload Instructions: Please follow upload instructions to attach all of the required supporting documents listed below.  Important: Do not use the Autofill feature when uploading your resume. Required Document: 1. Resume Optional Document (Recommended for Best Consideration): 2. Cover Letter detailing your qualifications for the position. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer paid 24 vacation days, 12 sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. We do the same for you.  In fact, we'll cover 100 percent of the tuition costs for you to earn your undergraduate degree and 80 percent of your graduate tuition.  This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Professional and download the spreadsheet. If you are applying for a 10-month position or one with Burris or Indiana Academy, please select the corresponding calculator."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/PT-Custodian_R-03416": {
      "title": "PT Custodian",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nPosition Title: PT Custodian Compensation: $18.33 - $18.33 Department: Student Center Start Date: 10/23/2026 About this opportunity: Ball State University welcomes applications for a part-time Custodian in the Student Center. We’re looking for a dependable Part-Time Custodian to help maintain a clean and welcoming environment. This position offers a steady weekend schedule: Friday, Saturday, Sunday from 3:00 PM to 11:30 PM. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function: Under supervision, perform custodial work required for the cleaning and maintenance of facilities following proper procedures and techniques for equipment and cleaning products. Duties and Responsibilities: 1. Clean, renovate, and renew, resilient, wood, and hard floor surface finishes, i.e., dust mop, sweep, wet mop, spot mop, scrub, polish and spray buff, strip, seal, wax, etc. Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, crawl, reach, balance, stand, walk, push, pull, lift, grasp, and exert up to 100 pounds of force occasionally, 50 pounds of force frequently, and 20 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, shape, shadow, distinguish colors, etc. Task requires the use of floor machines, wet/dry vacuum, scrubbers, mops, buckets, measuring cups, putty knife, cloth or sponge, eye protection, filter mask, and various chemical products, etc. Task exposes employee to inside environmental conditions, noise, chemicals, fumes, odors, dusts, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 2. Maintain carpet floor coverings, i.e., vacuum, remove spots and stains, extraction cleaning of carpet and upholstered furniture, etc. Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, crawl, reach, stand, walk, push, pull, lift, grasp, and exert up to 100 pounds of force occasionally, 50 pounds of force frequently, and 20 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, shape, shadow, distinguish colors, etc. Task requires the use of carpet vacuum, shampooer, wet/dry vacuum, extractor, putty knife, sponge or cloth, eye protection, and various chemical products, etc. Task exposes employee to inside environmental conditions, noise, chemicals, fumes, odors, dusts, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 3. Clean and service all areas in restrooms to include, but not limited to, lavatory, toilet, urinal, showers, bath tub areas, etc.   Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, reach, balance, stand, climb, walk, push, pull, lift, grasp, and exert up to 100 pounds of force occasionally, 50 pounds of force frequently, and 20 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, shape, shadow, distinguish colors, etc. Task requires the use of floor machine, wet/dry vacuum, portable vacuum, hand duster, bucket and wringer, mops, brushes, ladder, sponge, eye protection, gloves, and various chemical products, etc. Task exposes employee to inside environmental conditions, noise, chemicals, fumes, odors, dusts, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions, information. 4. Dust, vacuum, clean and/or polish furniture and surfaces; clean facilities, fixtures, hardware and windows; change lighting and clean fixtures as required.     Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, reach, balance, stand, climb, walk, push, pull, lift, grasp, and exert up to 100 pounds of force occasionally, 50 pounds of force frequently, and 20 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, shape, shadow, distinguish colors, etc. Task requires the use of dust mop, portable vacuum, hand duster, counter brush, ladder, scaffolds, screwdriver, spray bottle, bucket, sponge, cloth, eye protection, filter mask, light bulbs and tubes, various chemical products, etc. Task exposes employee to inside working conditions, noise, chemicals, fumes, odors, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 5. Collect and dispose of trash, refuse, recycling materials and debris into designated receptacles.      Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, reach, balance, walk, push, pull, lift, grasp, and exert up to 100 pounds of force occasionally, 50 pounds of force frequently, and 20 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, etc. Task requires the use of trash carts, bags and cans, dollies, gloves, etc. Task exposes employee to inside and outside environmental conditions, chemicals, fumes, odors, dusts, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 6. Sweep debris, and/or sweep or shovel snow from outside entrances, landings, steps, walks, etc.; clean exterior surfaces of windows, doors, and other building fixtures.      Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, reach, stand, walk, lift, push, pull, grasp, and exert up to 50 pounds of force occasionally, 25 pounds of force frequently, and 10 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, distinguish colors, etc. Task requires the use of brooms, snow shovel, plastic bags, squeegee, pole, rags, towels, etc. Task exposes employee to inside and outside working conditions. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 7. Acquire and maintain knowledge of job related chemicals, and environmental, health and physical concerns.      Requires the physical ability to exert up to 10 pounds of force occasionally. Task requires the mental ability to read, comprehend and follow: written instructions, procedures, precautions, etc., used in connection with custodial related work, trade publications, department handbook, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 8. Lock and unlock doors as required to perform job related duties in accordance with departmental policies and procedures.     Requires the physical ability to stoop, reach, stand, walk, and exert up to 10 pounds of force occasionally. Task requires the visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distance, shape, shadows, etc. Task requires the use of keys. Task exposes employee to inside and outside environmental conditions. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 9. Set up audio visual equipment in meeting rooms and lounges.      Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, reach, climb, stand, walk, lift, push, pull, grasp, and exert in excess of 50 pounds of force occasionally, 20 pounds of force frequently, and 10 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, shape, shadow, etc. Task requires the ability to operate VCR’s, monitors, cassette players, record players microphones, sound systems, VIS system and various other audio visual equipment. Task exposes employee to inside and outside environmental conditions, fumes, odors, dusts, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to read, comprehend and follow written directions and materials. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information the mental ability to use good judgement and discretion. 10. Guide, assist and train student custodial employees.      Requires the ability to use machines, tools and equipment, as well as the ability to achieve result through student employees, give clear directions, monitor performance and give feedback. Task requires the ability to communicate effectively with people on a one-on-one basis which includes clarity, organization of thought and expressiveness, write any necessary documentation, be detail-oriented and perform basic mathematical calculations. 11. Set rooms according to custodial worksheet and customer specification; arrange, move and/or replace furniture, supplies and miscellaneous equipment from room to room as required; report all items requiring repair to supervisor and/or group leaders; perform minor repairs to furniture and building fixtures.      Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, reach, climb, stand, walk, lift, push, pull, grasp, and exert in excess of 50 pounds of force occasionally, 20 pounds of force frequently, and 10 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, shape, shadow, etc. Task requires the mental ability to effectively communicate with people on a one-on-one basis which includes clarity organization of thought. Task requires the ability to use good judgement discretion, independence, and to read and comprehend computerized custodial worksheets. 12. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: 1. High School diploma or equivalent (GED). 2.No allergies to soaps, cleaning agents, chemicals or environmental factors in custodial service. 3.Must be able to climb ladders, scaffolding of various heights to perform duties. 4.Must be able to read and comprehend all specified policies and procedures for safety, hazardous materials information, University policies, procedures and communications, and perform basic mathematical functions. 5.Must possess and maintain a valid Drivers License, if required by assignment, and be insurable under the university's auto fleet policy. 6.Must be able to perform all mental and physical requirements as outlined in each job duty. 7. Must have at least one year work experience (full time or part time). 8.You must be 18 years or older. Preferred Qualifications: 1.Experience in custodial work. 2.Knowledge of audiovisual equipment. How to apply: You can reach out to studentctr@bsu.edu  for any questions. Please follow upload instructions to attach all of the supporting documents listed below: NOTE: Do not use the Autofill feature for resume. Cover Letter (optional) Resume (optional)"
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Scheduling-Specialist_R-03286": {
      "title": "Scheduling Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Scheduling Specialist Salary Grade: Staff 10 Compensation: $16.50 - 17.84 Department: TC Deans Office_E Start Date: 10/19/2026 About this opportunity: Join our team and play a key role in supporting the Teachers College! We are seeking a highly organized, detail-oriented professional to coordinate course scheduling, faculty workload, classroom assignments, and critical academic data. In this collaborative role, you’ll work closely with department chairs, faculty, and the Dean’s Office to help ensure courses are scheduled effectively, enrollment needs are addressed, and faculty assignments are accurate. If you enjoy problem-solving, working with data and technology, managing important deadlines, and partnering with others to keep complex processes running smoothly, this is an excellent opportunity to make a meaningful impact on the day-to-day operations of the college. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the United States without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function: Maintain accurate course and room schedule and faculty workload data in Banner. Serve as the college’s primary contact for all issues related to schedule and faculty load data. Coordinate and maintain data in Faculty Load and Compensation (FLAC) system. Prepare data and related reports for Associate Dean. Available during peak enrollment periods, which is typically the last and first four weeks of each semester. Duties & Responsibilities: 1. Monitor, troubleshoot and manage departmental and college course input into Banner. Work directly with department chairpersons in compiling faculty assignments (e.g. preliminary faculty loading reports, non-instructional load reports, final load reports, etc.) each semester in preparation for review and approval by the Dean’s Office; build and assign course load based on Dean’s Office oversight for faculty in Banner. Coordinate, enter and maintain data in FLAC; including entering instruction, non-instructional load and overloads into university systems. 2. Review and correct on-campus and off-campus schedule of classes each semester; review past course enrollments and individual program prerequisite enrollments to determine course needs. Verify credit hour values, dates of term, times and room assignments for courses; delete and/or add courses as necessary. Work with the Office of Teacher Education Services to create courses and add instructors of record. Closely monitor individual course enrollments and caps in consultation with the Dean’s Office; increase caps or open new sections as needed at the direction of the Dean’s Office. Check course offerings for conflicts with other courses offered at the university using Argos. Follow the University time configuration guidelines when creating courses to avoid student schedule conflicts. 3. Be aware and adhere to important semester and academic deadlines from Office of Registration and Student Records and the Office of Institutional Research and Decision Support. Deadlines include priority course registration and open registration dates, drop/add schedules, final grade postings, the assignment of general-purpose classrooms, classroom attribute deadlines, semester course roll schedules and final course build deadlines. 4. Work with the Dean’s Office to identify and problem solve enrollment issues related to full courses, establishing and monitoring wait lists, need for additional course sections, need to eliminate course sections, reassigning faculty when sections are cancelled and monitoring faculty FTE’s. 5. Assist in developing and monitoring data integrity standards for all schedule and faculty load related data. Specifically, create, maintain, and update relevant database systems and generate administrative reports, spreadsheets and lists of classes; update training documentation for course creation and disseminate changes to processes for inter-personnel. 6. Evaluate schedule requests for adherence to campus policies and procedures; contact departments for supplementary information and justifications, as necessary. Assist Dean’s Office in ensuring compliance with approved department course caps. Assist Dean’s Office in formulating requests for faculty overloads. 7. Provide support to Series 25 classroom space allocation and utilization software. Utilize Smart Catalog for pre-requisite requirements to determine course offering needs. 8. Attend trainings and workshops as needed related to position duties and responsibilities. 9. Maintain regular and predictable attendance. 10. Perform other related duties as assigned. Provide additional operational support for Teachers College Dean’s Office as needed. Minimum Qualifications: Associate’s degree or an additional two years of related experience will be accepted in lieu of an associate's degree. At least one year of office administrative, secretarial, or related experience.   Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. Some experience using Banner and R25 (i.e. room scheduling software). Familiarity with Degree Works, academic procedures and college calendars, strong communication skills, ability to work independently as well as collaboratively. How to apply: For inquiries about this opportunity, please contact Margo Akins: makins@bsu.edu Important: Please follow upload instructions for attaching all required documents listed below. Only complete applications will be considered. REQUIRED APPLICATION DOCUMENTS: Cover Letter Resume Within the application, please provide names and contact information for three (3) professional references. Application Deadline: October 19, 2026, 12:01 a.m. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer paid 10 to 24 vacation days, 12 sick days, and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. We do the same for you.  In fact, we'll cover 100 percent of the tuition costs for you to earn your undergraduate degree and 80 percent of your graduate tuition.  This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that show you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Staff (Exempt) and download our calculator spreadsheet."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Assistant-Teaching-Professor-of-History_R-03271": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor of History",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Assistant Teaching Professor of History Compensation: $45,000 - $55,000 Department: History Start Date: 08/01/2027 About this opportunity: Ball State University welcomes applications for a non-tenure-track position to teach four in-person classes per semester. The primary area of study is European Union/European Integration. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you will bring: Duties and Responsibilities: Teaching undergraduate survey courses and upper-division courses as necessary. Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. by date of appointment Teaching experience at the university level in European history. Experience with visual history, including games, video documentaries, cultural analytics of images, image analysis, augmented reality, or virtual reality. Preferred Qualifications: PhD. in 20th century European history   How to apply: For inquiries, please contact: Ronald V. Morris at rvmorris@bsu.edu Please follow upload instructions to attach all of the required supporting documents listed below: NOTE: Do not use the Autofill feature for resume: Cover Letter CV Student evaluations (scores and comments) Consideration Date: to ensure full consideration, applications must be received by November 23, 2026. (Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants.) What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer 12 paid sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Faculty and download our calculator spreadsheet."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Administrative-Coordinator-and-Secretary-for-University-Governance_R-03358": {
      "title": "Administrative Coordinator and Secretary for University Governance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Administrative Coordinator and Secretary for University Governance Salary Grade: Staff 10 Compensation: $16.50 - 17.84 Department: Vice Provost Academic Affairs_B Start Date: 11/09/2026 About this opportunity: The Office of the Vice Provost for Academic Affairs is searching for an enthusiastic and detail-oriented person to join our team. This position is responsible for coordinating governance operations and supporting a transparent, flexible, responsive, and efficient governance system, as well as providing administrative support for the Office of the Vice Provost. Our unit is committed to maintaining a positive and collaborative work environment, where your success is supported. This is a full-time opportunity that comes with a great benefits package, including generous paid time off and paid holidays, University-funded retirement programs, medical and prescription plans, dental, flexible spending and health savings accounts, tuition discounts, and much more! Come be a part of our team! This position requires a full-time on-campus presence. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the United States without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function: Coordinate university governance operations and provide administrative support for the Vice Provost for Academic Affairs. Manage governance calendars, meetings, elections, and records in collaboration with governance leadership and the VPAA Office to support a transparent, flexible, responsive, and efficient governance system. Provide in-person administrative support for governance committee meetings as well as to the VPAA office, including performing VPAA reception duties, managing correspondence, and assisting with events and special projects that advance the University's strategic goals. Duties & Responsibilities: 1. Coordinate governance operations: in consultation with governance leadership and the VPAA office, plan and organize meetings, calendars, and logistics for University Senate, Senate Agenda Committee, Governance and Elections Committee, Faculty Council, Professional Personnel Council, and other governance committees as well as assigned ad hoc committees. 2. Prepare and manage documentation: in consultation with governance leadership and the VPAA office, prepare and distribute agendas and meeting materials; collect and maintain physical and digital records of agenda items, minutes, reports, memberships lists, and other governance records as directed; ensure digital records are searchable and accessible as appropriate. 3. Provide logistics support: attend meetings and provide logistical support for the University Senate, Senate Agenda Committee, Governance and Elections Committee, Faculty Council, Professional Personnel Council, and other assigned committees, including preparing meeting rooms, setting up technology, and assisting Council and/or committee leaders as needed. Oversee the Governance email account and respond promptly to any inquiries. 4. Maintain governance websites and Handbook: update and oversee University Senate and committee websites and shared information/training sites; ensure the Faculty and Professional Handbook is accurate and current and that it provides guidance and information on University policy as approved by Board action. 5. Administer Governance elections and support committee operations: manage faculty and professional personnel election and appointment processes; collaborate with colleges, departments, and Student Government leadership to ensure vacancies are filled and procedures are followed. Track membership, attendance and annual reports for governance committees; distribute appointment letters; ensure committees fulfill required reporting and documentation. 6. Facilitate communication and engagement: generate and distribute preference polls, announcements, and governance updates; build positive relationships to foster campus-wide participation in shared governance. Assist with Executive Reporting as requested, including monitoring paperwork and workflow of all governance actions, preparing summaries for presidential and Board of Trustees review, and assisting the Senate Chair in preparing reports. 7. Provide support for professional development: coordinate and support professional development opportunities that enhance faculty and staff understanding of university governance and shared decision-making. Assist in the planning and implementation of professional development events and special projects that advance the university's strategic goals as directed. 8 . Provide administrative support to the Director of the University Core Curriculum as directed, including updating the Core Curriculum website and other Core Curriculum workflow and digital materials; providing support for programming, professional development, and student success activities related to the Core Curriculum; designing materials for admissions, advisors, and faculty in support of the Core Curriculum; and attending meetings, taking minutes, and organizing information for the Director. 9. Provide additional secretarial and administrative support for the Vice Provost of Academic Affairs office as appropriate. 10 . Perform other related duties as assigned. 11 . Maintain regular and predictable attendance. Minimum Qualifications:    Associate’s degree or one of the following: 1. An equivalent combination of two years of education and related experience in office administration/secretarial work or related field OR 2. Two years of related experience in office administration/secretarial work, or related field. At least one year of office administration/secretarial experience; proficiency with Microsoft Office applications; must have strong written and oral communication skills; must have strong organizational skills and a keen attention to detail; ability to manage multiple tasks, meet deadlines, and work independently with minimal supervision Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. Knowledge of university governance structures, policies, and procedures Experience working in an academic, governance, or higher education environment Familiarity with Banner, Canvas, and/or Qualtrics Experience with web page creation and maintenance How to apply: Please follow upload instructions to attach the required documents listed below. Note: do not use the Autofill function for the resume: 1. Cover Letter - required 2. Resume - required (do not use the Autofill resume feature) Applications must be submitted by 12:01 AM on October 21, 2026. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer paid 10 to 24 vacation days, 12 sick days, and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. We do the same for you.  In fact, we'll cover 100 percent of the tuition costs for you to earn your undergraduate degree and 80 percent of your graduate tuition.  This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that show you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Staff (Exempt) and download our calculator spreadsheet."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Instructors-of-Music--Academic-_R-02363": {
      "title": "Instructors of Music (Academic)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nPosition Title:   Instructors of Music (Academic) Compensation: $500 - $9,000 Department: School of Music - Temp Start Date: 08/17/2026 About this opportunity:   The School of Music at Ball State University invites applications for ongoing part-time semester instructor appointments to teach coursework in an on-campus environment. Our faculty are expected to be innovative, adaptive, engaging teachers across modalities; producers of impactful creative and/or scholarly work; leaders in meaningful service activities to multiple communities. We seek faculty members who are strategic thinkers, engaging our dynamic communities. We are committed to student and faculty success, impact, and access. We inspire each other to be critical thinkers and responsible citizens, so that we can pursue fulfilling careers and meaningful lives. Ball State University lives the values of Beneficence. We invite you to be part of our community. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring:   Position Description: This is a part time semester instructor position in an on campus environment. Job Duties and Responsibilities: Primary assignment will initially include teaching coursework in music education, music history, music theory/composition, or music technology. Other duties as assigned by the Director of the School of Music. Minimum Qualifications: Completed master’s degree and a documented history of professional activity in the area of assignment. Demonstrated ability to recruit and retain quality students on the collegiate level, exceptional creative/scholarly background. How to apply:   IMPORTANT: To ensure accurate processing of your application, do not rely on a resume upload to populate the job experience section. All work history must be entered directly into the application fields. Required Documents 1. Cover Letter 2. Curriculum Vitae (Do Not use the \"Autofill\" resume feature.) Optional Documents 1. Master's Transcripts 2. Doctorate Transcripts Search Committee Chair Name and Contact Information: Dr. Franklin Larey, Director School of Music, music@bsu.edu Applications will be accepted through November 20, 2026, at 12:01 AM. The option to upload transcripts is available. Original, official transcripts showing the highest related degree earned is required at the time of hire (even if obtained at BSU). Degree verification will be conducted."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Instructor-of-Music--Applied-_R-01872": {
      "title": "Instructor of Music (Applied)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nPosition Title:   Instructor of Music (Applied) Compensation: $500 - $9,000 Department: School of Music - Temp Start Date: 08/17/2026 About this opportunity:   The School of Music at Ball State University invites applications for ongoing part-time semester faculty appointments to teach applied music coursework in an on-campus environment. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring:   Job Description: The primary assignment will initially include teaching applied lessons, coaching chamber music, or conducting ensembles, along with other duties as assigned by the Director of the School of Music. Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree from an accredited college or university and a documented history of professional activity in the area of assignment. Demonstrated ability to recruit and retain quality students on the collegiate level, exceptional creative/scholarly background. How to apply:   Required Documents: 1. Cover Letter 2. Curriculum Vitae (Do Not use the \"Autofill\" resume feature) Optional Documents: 1. Master's Transcripts 2. Doctorate Transcripts For inquiries regarding these opportunities, please contact: Dr. Franklin Larey, Director School of Music, music@bsu.edu Applications will be accepted through November 20, 2026 at 12:01 AM."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Assistant-Teaching-Professor-in-Music--Media--Production_R-03355": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor in Music, Media, Production",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Assistant Teaching Professor in Music, Media, Production Compensation: $55,000 - $60,000 Department: School of Music Start Date: 08/16/2027 About this opportunity: The School of Music invites applications for a non-tenure line faculty position in Music Media Production with an emphasis on live sound, recording technology, and professional audio-visual systems. The successful candidate will bring substantial industry experience and demonstrated expertise in contemporary recording, live production, and AV workflows. Responsibilities will include teaching undergraduate courses in recording technology, live sound, audio systems, and related production areas, as well as supervising senior projects. We seek a practitioner-educator who can provide authentic, hands-on instruction and prepare students for professional work across recording, live sound, AV systems integration, and related areas of the audio industry. Our dynamic faculty function as collaborative, innovative, and adaptive members of the College of Fine Arts community, both within and beyond the School of Music, dedicated as teachers, productive in creative and/or scholarly work, engaged in public service activities, and contributing to the Ball State and Muncie communities and beyond. Additionally, lifelong learning at Ball State University extends educational engagement beyond the classroom and includes learning for professional development, personal enrichment, or other goals throughout one’s lifetime. Faculty will be expected to participate in the department’s, college’s, and university’s lifelong learning opportunities when available and may be assigned teaching responsibility in this area as part of their overall responsibilities. Candidates must have current authorization to be employed in the US without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you will bring: Duties and Responsibilities: Instructional load will include all levels of course work in classes such as: teaching undergraduate courses in recording technology, live sound, audio systems, and related production areas, as well as supervising senior projects. Other teaching duties based on programmatic need and the expertise of the faculty hired individual may be assigned Demonstrated commitment to student success Mentorship / project / capstone oversight Live sound and studio production Minimum Qualifications: Education: Master’s degree in music technology, audio engineering or a related field OR Bachelor’s degree and significant industry achievements or experience Experience: Music technology instruction at the University level Expert level proficiency or certifications in AVID Pro Tools Ultimate including AVID S series of control surfaces Expert level proficiency in analog and digital audio equipment Expert level proficiency in basic analog signal flow Expert level proficiency in live sound reinforcement and AV applications Preferred Qualifications: Education: Ph.D. or equivalent in music technology, audio engineering, DSP Experience: Significant industry and/or teaching experience in immersive audio, DSP, and cross-discipline collaboration. Demonstrated experience in game audio, immersive environments. Demonstrated excellence in teaching undergraduate courses. Experience in live sound, and production of commercial music. Demonstrated high proficiency on industry standard work surfaces such as SSL inline consoles, AVID, and Yamaha. Experience in electronics, soldering, building, instruction How to apply: Please direct inquiries regarding this vacancy to Dr. Franklin Larey, Director of the School of Music, franklin.larey@bsu.edu The following application materials are required: Cover letter of application Curriculum vita e In addition, applicants are asked to provide the contact information of at least three referees. Consideration Date: to ensure full consideration, applications must be received by December 1, 2026. (Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants.) What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer 12 paid sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Faculty and download our calculator spreadsheet. Relocation Program: MakeMyMove   Looking for a place where your career, community, and quality of life can grow together? Muncie and Delaware County offer the advantages of a university community with the accessibility and affordability of a mid-sized city—short commutes, local arts and culture, parks and trails, educational opportunities, healthcare, athletics, and a strong sense of connection. Through the City of Muncie’s MakeMyMove program, eligible new residents may access relocation incentives, university-related benefits, co-working options, and community-building opportunities designed to help them get settled and connected. Current benefits for eligible participants may include: Up to $5,000 cash for relocation Co-working space courtesy of The Innovation Connector Access to the Ball State University Library One-semester Rec Fit pass at Ball State University Discounted Ball State Athletics tickets Help desk for connecting to local resources Opportunity for board participation Six-month YMCA membership Relocation programs on MakeMyMove have specific eligibility requirements, and benefits may vary by program. Applicants should review the current MakeMyMove page to confirm eligibility, available benefits, and program status. Ball State’s support for relocation resources and community partnerships aligns with Destination 2040: Our Flight Path , the University’s strategic plan. The plan calls our University to work with community partners to promote talent attraction and retention, foster economic growth in East Central Indiana, and strengthen the economic and social vitality of the communities we serve. Explore the program and check your eligibility through MakeMyMove ."
